Relevant tables / columns for the analysis  

tree_census_1995  
- `spc_latin`: Latin (scientific) name – will be upper‑cased and filtered for non‑empty values.  
- `spc_common`: Common name – to be displayed together with latin name.  
- `status`: Condition of the tree – will be grouped into Alive / Dead buckets (e.g. ‘Alive’, ‘Good’, ‘Poor’ → Alive; ‘Dead’ → Dead).  
- `recordid`: Always present – convenient unique key for counting.  

tree_census_2005 / 2015 – same columns and same usage as above.  

tree_species  
- `species_scientific_name`: Canonical Latin name – can be joined to tidy spelling.  
- `species_common_name`: Canonical common name – for extra description.  

Typical conditions  
•  `spc_latin IS NOT NULL AND spc_latin <> ''` – keep only rows that have a Latin name.  
•  `UPPER(status) IN ('ALIVE','GOOD','POOR','EXCELLENT')`  → Alive bucket.  
•  `UPPER(status) IN ('DEAD','STUMP')` → Dead bucket.  
•  Year flag is implicit in table name, so no date filtering is required.  

Below are ten exploratory → increasingly complex BigQuery SQL snippets (all limited to 20 rows) that one would run while moving from raw inspection to the final answer.

```sql
-- 1. Inspect the raw status values that exist in 1995
SELECT DISTINCT status
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_census_1995`
WHERE status IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Inspect the raw status values that exist in 2005
SELECT DISTINCT status
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_census_2005`
WHERE status IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect the raw status values that exist in 2015
SELECT DISTINCT status
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_census_2015`
WHERE status IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Peek at Latin vs. common name pairs in the 1995 census
SELECT UPPER(spc_latin) AS latin_name,
       spc_common      AS common_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_census_1995`
WHERE spc_latin IS NOT NULL AND spc_latin <> ''
GROUP BY latin_name, common_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Quick count of total trees per Latin species in 1995
SELECT UPPER(spc_latin) AS latin_name,
       COUNT(*)         AS total_1995
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_census_1995`
WHERE spc_latin IS NOT NULL AND spc_latin <> ''
GROUP BY latin_name
ORDER BY total_1995 DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Alive vs Dead buckets for 1995 (simple mapping)
SELECT UPPER(spc_latin) AS latin_name,
       SUM(CASE WHEN UPPER(status) IN ('DEAD','STUMP') THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS dead_1995,
       SUM(CASE WHEN UPPER(status) NOT IN ('DEAD','STUMP') THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS alive_1995
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_census_1995`
WHERE spc_latin IS NOT NULL AND spc_latin <> ''
GROUP BY latin_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Equivalent alive / dead breakdown for 2015
SELECT UPPER(spc_latin) AS latin_name,
       SUM(CASE WHEN UPPER(status) IN ('DEAD','STUMP') THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS dead_2015,
       SUM(CASE WHEN UPPER(status) NOT IN ('DEAD','STUMP') THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS alive_2015
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_census_2015`
WHERE spc_latin IS NOT NULL AND spc_latin <> ''
GROUP BY latin_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Join 1995 and 2015 counts to see growth or decline
WITH c1995 AS (
  SELECT UPPER(spc_latin) AS latin_name,
         COUNT(*) AS total_1995
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_census_1995`
  WHERE spc_latin IS NOT NULL AND spc_latin <> ''
  GROUP BY latin_name
),
c2015 AS (
  SELECT UPPER(spc_latin) AS latin_name,
         COUNT(*) AS total_2015
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_census_2015`
  WHERE spc_latin IS NOT NULL AND spc_latin <> ''
  GROUP BY latin_name
)
SELECT c2015.latin_name,
       total_1995,
       total_2015,
       total_2015 - total_1995 AS diff_total
FROM c2015
JOIN c1995 USING(latin_name)
ORDER BY diff_total DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Pull canonical common names from tree_species to compare spelling
SELECT DISTINCT UPPER(ts.species_scientific_name) AS latin_name,
       ts.species_common_name                    AS canonical_common
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_species` AS ts
WHERE ts.species_scientific_name IS NOT NULL AND ts.species_scientific_name <> ''
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final‑style query assembling counts & growth (alive / dead / totals) between 1995 and 2015
WITH base_1995 AS (
  SELECT UPPER(spc_latin) AS latin_name,
         spc_common,
         COUNT(*) AS total_1995,
         SUM(CASE WHEN UPPER(status) IN ('DEAD','STUMP') THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)  AS dead_1995,
         SUM(CASE WHEN UPPER(status) NOT IN ('DEAD','STUMP') THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS alive_1995
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_census_1995`
  WHERE spc_latin IS NOT NULL AND spc_latin <> ''
  GROUP BY latin_name, spc_common
),
base_2015 AS (
  SELECT UPPER(spc_latin) AS latin_name,
         COUNT(*) AS total_2015,
         SUM(CASE WHEN UPPER(status) IN ('DEAD','STUMP') THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)  AS dead_2015,
         SUM(CASE WHEN UPPER(status) NOT IN ('DEAD','STUMP') THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS alive_2015
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_census_2015`
  WHERE spc_latin IS NOT NULL AND spc_latin <> ''
  GROUP BY latin_name
),
merged AS (
  SELECT b15.latin_name,
         b95.spc_common                                         AS common_name,
         total_1995, total_2015,
         alive_1995, alive_2015,
         dead_1995,  dead_2015,
         total_2015 - total_1995 AS diff_total,
         alive_2015 - alive_1995 AS diff_alive,
         dead_2015  - dead_1995  AS diff_dead
  FROM base_1995 b95
  JOIN base_2015 b15 USING(latin_name)
)
SELECT latin_name,
       common_name,
       total_1995, alive_1995, dead_1995,
       total_2015, alive_2015, dead_2015,
       diff_total, diff_alive, diff_dead
FROM merged
ORDER BY diff_total DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

The above ten queries progress from basic data inspection to a full calculation of growth differences for the required “top 10” species.
