Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables / columns for the analysis  

tree_census_1995  
- `spc_latin`: Latin (scientific) name – will be upper‑cased and filtered for non‑empty values.  
- `spc_common`: Common name – to be displayed together with latin name.  
- `status`: Condition of the tree – will be grouped into Alive / Dead buckets (e.g. ‘Alive’, ‘Good’, ‘Poor’ → Alive; ‘Dead’ → Dead).  
- `recordid`: Always present – convenient unique key for counting.  

tree_census_2005 / 2015 – same columns and same usage as above.  

tree_species  
- `species_scientific_name`: Canonical Latin name – can be joined to tidy spelling.  
- `species_common_name`: Canonical common name – for extra description.  

Typical conditions  
•  `spc_latin IS NOT NULL AND spc_latin <> ''` – keep only rows that have a Latin name.  
•  `UPPER(status) IN ('ALIVE','GOOD','POOR','EXCELLENT')`  → Alive bucket.  
•  `UPPER(status) IN ('DEAD','STUMP')` → Dead bucket.  
•  Year flag is implicit in table name, so no date filtering is required.  

Below are ten exploratory → increasingly complex BigQuery SQL snippets (all limited to 20 rows) that one would run while moving from raw inspection to the final answer.

```sql
-- 1. Inspect the raw status values that exist in 1995
SELECT DISTINCT status
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_census_1995`
WHERE status IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Inspect the raw status values that exist in 2005
SELECT DISTINCT status
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_census_2005`
WHERE status IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect the raw status values that exist in 2015
SELECT DISTINCT status
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_census_2015`
WHERE status IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Peek at Latin vs. common name pairs in the 1995 census
SELECT UPPER(spc_latin) AS latin_name,
       spc_common      AS common_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tree_census_1995`
WHERE spc_latin IS NOT NULL AND spc_latin <> ''
GROUP BY latin_name, common_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
